payload.message:
Use **Function calling (client execution)** for your setup.

It means OCI/model decides:

```text
call function: route_request
with args: {...}
```

But **your client/Zeroclaw executes it**, not OCI. That is what you want for tag routing.

Use a small schema like this:

```json
{
  "name": "route_request",
  "description": "Classify a user request into an internal routing tag and execution mode.",
  "parameters": {
    "type": "object",
    "additionalProperties": false,
    "properties": {
      "tag": {
        "type": "string",
        "enum": [
          "dns",
          "mail",
          "xray",
          "zeroclaw",
          "ui_generation",
          "identity",
          "container",
          "unknown"
        ]
      },
      "confidence": {
        "type": "number",
        "minimum": 0,
        "maximum": 1
      },
      "intent": {
        "type": "string"
      },
      "needs_execution": {
        "type": "boolean"
      }
    },
    "required": ["tag", "confidence", "intent", "needs_execution"]
  }
}
```

For UI generation, add a second function:

```json
{
  "name": "generate_ui_spec",
  "description": "Create a compact UI specification for rendering an operational control surface.",
  "parameters": {
    "type": "object",
    "additionalProperties": false,
    "properties": {
      "title": { "type": "string" },
      "view_type": {
        "type": "string",
        "enum": ["dashboard", "form", "table", "inspector", "wizard"]
      },
      "components": {
        "type": "array",
        "items": {
          "type": "object",
          "additionalProperties": true
        }
      }
    },
    "required": ["title", "view_type", "components"]
  }
}
```

Keep the functions boring and narrow. Let the model classify and produce structured args; let your local code decide what is allowed to run.

